WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.180
This is Puerto Rico on a calm night in July
2017.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.420
Here it is again, after Hurricane Maria in
September.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.820
The storm's impact has been catastrophic.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.570
It was at its strongest when it was passing
over the most populated parts of the island,

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.680
which is home to about 3.4 million people.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.680
The impact has been catastrophic.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.499
It knocked off Puerto Rico’s power grid
and now it’ll be months before most of the

00:00:22.499 --> 00:00:24.460
island has electricity again.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.909
What’s made recovery particularly hard is
that the government has no money and that’s

00:00:29.909 --> 00:00:35.320
partly because of its complex relationship
with the US mainland.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.620
Puerto Rico became a US commonwealth in 1952.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:44.350
But nearly half of all Americans don’t know
that people born in Puerto Rico are U.S. citizens.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.540
The island’s economic downturn can be traced
to the 1960s and 70s, when a special tax break

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.120
from Congress led US companies to set up shop
in Puerto Rico.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.090
Then, between 1993 and 2006, Congress phased
out those tax breaks, and companies left the

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:02.739
island in droves, taking thousands of jobs
with them.

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:06.900
Economic growth slowed to a crawl, and hordes
of people started leaving the island.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.680
The number of Puerto Ricans living in mainland
U.S. doubled between 2005 and 2015.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.820
As the tax base shrank, Puerto Rico went into
massive debt to pay its bills.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.590
Today, it owes more than $70 billion, mostly
to Wall Street creditors

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:26.290
In May of 2017, it filed for protection similar
to a bankruptcy.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.980
Budgets for hospitals, schools, and roads
were slashed.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:34.520
Another US policy that partly led to PR’s
economic turmoil is the Merchant Marine Act

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.920
of 1920, or the Jones Act.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.790
It places huge tariffs on any foreign imports.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:44.100
And although President Trump suspended the
Jones Act in the wake of hurricane Maria,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.151
these costs have been passed on to Puerto
Ricans for decades.

00:01:48.151 --> 00:01:53.230
Since Puerto Rico imports 85% of its food,
people often pay double for food and other

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.230
basic necessities.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:59.420
The high cost of living is one of the reasons
43% of residents live in poverty.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.840
Crucially, the island’s financial woes have
kept it from investing in the kind of modern,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.650
automated power plant technology that’s
characteristic of the mainland US.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.450
That's going to make recovery difficult.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.860
Most power plants in the mainland rely on
natural gas, with some coal, nuclear, and

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:15.860
renewables.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.510
But Puerto Rico’s old-fashioned plants still
generate two-thirds of the power from burning

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:20.510
oil.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.270
And all that oil has to be imported.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.310
In the aftermath of hurricane Maria, the power
plants are mostly intact.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.710
But nearly 80 percent of the transmission
lines that carry power are down.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.880
And so are the roads that bring oil to the
power plants.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.370
While the island’s power plants are on the
southern coast, most of the people live in

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.370
the north.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.060
And between those two ends sit dense forests
and mountains.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.670
Apart from the challenging terrain, Puerto
Rico’s electric utility company, PREPA,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:51.990
relies heavily on workers with extensive knowledge
— and those workers have been leaving.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:56.650
Since 2012, 30 percent of PREPA’s employees
have retired or left for the mainland in search

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:58.330
of better jobs.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:03.060
Electrical engineers from Puerto Rico can
make about 27 percent more money on the mainland.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.490
According to its director, “PREPA is on
the verge of collapse because there’s no

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.090
personnel to operate it.”

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.180
And that was before the storm.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.240
Without technicians to repair all the broken
transmission lines, Puerto Ricans are expected

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.650
to be without power for months.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.120
And the consequences of that can be dire.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.650
Without electricity to pump water into homes,
it’s difficult to find water for drinking,

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:24.650
bathing.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.200
No air conditioning or fans can mean increased
risk of heatstroke.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.560
And no refrigerators to keep insulin from
expiring.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:32.820
People are at risk of dying.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:38.709
All of this means millions of US citizens,
for the foreseeable future, will be living

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:42.780
in conditions we usually associate with places
very far away.

